A||N||3||mad dog||vibration & steering noise||||1||
Z||000000||mad dog||05-16-2000||02:59 PM||||86 Fiero se has excessive vibration at highway speeds.  It has had shocks,struts,tires,alignment,& balance done but problem persists.  Any suggestions?<P>Also, when turning steering wheel,it now makes noise (turning either way) at diff speeds.  Local mechanics have no clue.  Steering Dampner was replaced last year.  Any suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||05-16-2000||03:45 PM||||<BR>Mad Dog, good to have you on the forum.<P>Sounds like most of the regular things have been checked.  But also check your front bearings, when were they last packed or replaced.<P>When were your rotors turned, they could be warped, uneven or too thin.<P>It is also possible that the calipers are giving you problems - make sure they move freely on their pins and are not binding.<P>Your tie rod ends could be worn, as well as the ball joints.<P>It is quite possible that something might be bent from a collision with a curb, pot hole or the like. This is assuming that you are not the original owner, an original owner would most likely know if they hit something that might bend the front suspension.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||05-16-2000||06:17 PM||||I had the same problem.  Turns out that one of the tires on my 15 inch rims (new to this car) was out of round.  Nothing you can do short of replacing the tires.  The guy at Big-O tires gave me a deal on some 205/55R15s that he had pulled, $50 installed.  A lot less vibration, but I think one of the rear tires is out of round as well and transmitting some vibration from the rear of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-17-2000||09:52 AM||||You checked to make sure it's not draging a rotor shield?<P>You can take out a big chunk of guessing by tapping the brakes just hard enough to drag the pads while turning.<P>When you turn the front brake hoses can pull the caliper enough to make noises as it <BR>drags a pad on the rotor. This is easy if the calipers are not installed right or the rubber bushings in them are bad. <P>I have one that does this now... Annoying more than anything else. I'll fix it along with the rest of the front brake work it needs over the summer. there's a rotor warping a little too.<P>pushing the brakes while turning may also help isolate if the noise is in the bearings, but don't count on it. (Hitting the breaks changes loading angles on the bearings.)<P>To make sure it's really the front and not the rear, try pulling the hand brake a bit. (Keep release button depressed.) <P>Obviously you need to do this in a parking lot or something. Not out on the road. especially the hand brake test.||||reg||1
